{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issues_index.json\\\")); ns={3891,370,2176,3701,3284}; [(print(\\\"---\\\",x.get(\\\"number\\\"),x.get(\\\"title\\\"),x.get(\\\"state\\\"),\\\"---\\\"),print(x.get(\\\"body\\\",\\\"\\\"))) for x in d if x.get(\\\"number\\\") in ns]'\"","aggregated_output":"--- 370 [REQUEST] print: print every arguments on the same line None ---\nHave you checked the issues for a similar suggestions? Yes\r\n\r\n**How would you improve Rich?**\r\n\r\nHi,\r\n\r\nIs it possible to print all values given to `rich.print` including data structures on the same line?\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n```python\r\nfrom rich import print\r\nprint('a', {'b': 'c'})\r\n```\r\n\r\noutput:\r\n```\r\na\r\n{'b': 'c'}\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs it possible to make it outputs\r\n```\r\na {'b': 'c'}\r\n```\r\n\r\nOf course while still highlighting `{'b': 'c'}`.\r\n\r\nI have tried setting `soft_wrap`, etc. with no effect.\r\n\r\n**What problem does it solved for you?**\r\n\r\nIt is much easier to scan for the output if they are put on the same line, when there are multiple outputs lines.\r\n\r\nExample\r\n```\r\nINSERT {'value': 100}\r\nDELETE {'value': 200}\r\n```\r\n\r\nCompared to\r\n```\r\nINSERT\r\n{'value': 100}\r\nDELETE\r\n{'value': 200}\r\n```\r\n\n--- 2176 Prevent context manager to overwrite previous one in jupyter None ---\nWhen in Jupyter lab, if I call independent instances of a `Progress()`, for example by mapping a function that invokes a `Progress()` through an iterable, the context manager overwrites/deletes previous instances along the iteration. \r\n\r\nFor example (inside a Jupyter Lab notebook) inspired from code in the tutorial:\r\n\r\n```\r\nimport time\r\n\r\nfrom rich.progress import Progress\r\n\r\ndef run_snippet(iteration):\r\n    with Progress() as progress:\r\n\r\n        task1 = progress.add_task(\"[red]Downloading...\", total=5)\r\n        while not progress.finished:\r\n            progress.update(task1, advance=0.5)\r\n            #progress.log(f'this iteration {iteration}') <- uncomment for \"in-context\" effect\r\n            time.sleep(0.2)\r\n            \r\nlist(map(run_snippet, range(10)))\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe code above will overwrite any print-out (or anything else like plots or rich.renderables) from previous iterations … even within the same context (for example by un-commenting the `.log()` line ). This behavior doesn't happen when executed from a console. There, anything that is printed or rendered, remains visible and piles up.\r\n\r\nMy ignorant guess is that when the next `Process()` context is invoked, the way jupyter handles it by just throwing clearing away what was displayed in the past and moves forward.\r\n\r\nIs there a way to prevent his?\r\n\r\n\r\nAnd finally, thanks so much for this wonderful tool!\r\n\r\n(Edited some words for clarity.)\r\n(Edit2: updated to specify that case has been tested only on Jupyter lab instead of Jupyter notebook)\n--- 3284 Is there a way to print the line number while console.log'ging a single line? None ---\nI was recommended rich's `console.log()` as a way to add to a `print` statement output the number of the line of the `print` statement itself. With `console.log(some_array)` the number of it's line is indeed being printed — after a column next to filename. But when I `console.log()` a single string there is no line number being printed. Is there a way to print one?\n--- 3701 [BUG] Behavior of console.print() with empty *args is different from regular print() None ---\n**Describe the bug**\n\nExpected that `console.print(end=\"!\")` will print only `!` as regular `python print()`, but it print line break without ending symbol.\n\n```python\nfrom rich.console import Console\n\nconsole = Console()\n\nfor f in (\n    lambda: print(\"A\", end=\"!\"),\n    lambda: print(end=\"!\"),\n    lambda: console.print(\"A\", end=\"!\"),\n    lambda: console.print(end=\"!\"),\n):\n    print(\"=>\", end=\"\")\n    f()\n    print(\"<=\")\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\n❯ python3 test.py\n=>A!<=\n=>!<=\n=>A!<=\n=>\n<=\n```\n\nrich == 14.0.0\n--- 3891 [BUG] print() return value is error。 None ---\n```python\nfrom rich import print\n```\nReplace built-in print function  to  rich.print\n\nwhen obj is complex objects like \n```python\nfrom datetime import date\nd = date.fromisoformat(\"2025-08-09\")\nl = [[d,1,1],[d,2,2]]\n```\n_The return value of the built-in print function is_\n```python\n>>> print(f\"ok {l}\")\nok [[datetime.date(2025, 8, 9), 1, 1], [datetime.date(2025, 8, 9), 2, 2]]\n```\n**the return value of the rich.print is** \n```python\n>>> from rich import print\n>>> print(f\"ok {l}\")\nok [, ]\n```\nno f-string ,only print is right\n```python\n>>> print(l)\n[[datetime.date(2025, 8, 9), 1, 1], [datetime.date(2025, 8, 9), 2, 2]]\n```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
